1
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:13,892
So, we just went over load
balancing in general.

2
00:00:13,892 --> 00:00:21,890
Now we'll get into the specific
utility for Linux ipvs.

3
00:00:21,890 --> 00:00:27,205
So, ipvs and ipvsadm.

4
00:00:27,205 --> 00:00:31,091
So, here's link to the man page.

5
00:00:31,091 --> 00:00:36,718
But just to distinguish these two, and
I kind of use them interchangeably,

6
00:00:36,718 --> 00:00:42,630
but ipvs implements transport layer
load balancing inside the Linux kernel.

7
00:00:42,630 --> 00:00:46,564
And Ipvasadm is used to set up,
maintain, or

8
00:00:46,564 --> 00:00:51,336
inspect the virtual server
table in the Linux kernel.

9
00:00:51,336 --> 00:00:57,504
So this is the actual command that we'll
be using and this is the service inside

10
00:00:57,504 --> 00:01:03,491
of the kernel which handles the packet
processing to do the load balancing.

11
00:01:03,491 --> 00:01:09,253
Now, you must have the ipvs
kernel module loaded,

12
00:01:09,253 --> 00:01:13,946
and you can load it with modprobe ip_vs.

13
00:01:13,946 --> 00:01:19,108
So, when you now want to create
Linux as a load balancer

14
00:01:19,108 --> 00:01:23,594
to a set of servers,
there's two main parts.

15
00:01:23,594 --> 00:01:27,708
So the first is you have
to define the service.

16
00:01:27,708 --> 00:01:32,166
So what IP address is it,
what port number is it, is it TCP,

17
00:01:32,166 --> 00:01:34,140
all of that kind of stuff.

18
00:01:34,140 --> 00:01:37,066
What scheduling algorithm
we're going to use.

19
00:01:37,066 --> 00:01:41,175
And the second part is
to define the servers.

20
00:01:41,175 --> 00:01:45,516
So we talked about the set
of servers is important and

21
00:01:45,516 --> 00:01:51,944
that's what we're going to, so these
are the two parts that we have to define.

22
00:01:51,944 --> 00:01:53,976
So, let's define the service.

23
00:01:53,976 --> 00:01:58,944
So, shown here, ipvs, and
then here's from the kind

24
00:01:58,944 --> 00:02:03,377
of structure of the command,
from the man page.

25
00:02:03,377 --> 00:02:08,036
And what we're going to do is we're
going to walk through each part

26
00:02:08,036 --> 00:02:13,069
individually and we're going to
have a running example down below.

27
00:02:13,069 --> 00:02:16,619
So once you see it kind
of broken down this way,

28
00:02:16,619 --> 00:02:22,192
it makes it real simple to kind of define
these and look at them on your own.

29
00:02:22,192 --> 00:02:25,766
So, the first part is the command.

30
00:02:25,766 --> 00:02:32,449
So here we're going to talk about if we
want to add, edit, or delete a service.

31
00:02:32,449 --> 00:02:37,020
So, recall,
the service is the public facing or

32
00:02:37,020 --> 00:02:42,931
the outward facing single IP
address that clients connect to,

33
00:02:42,931 --> 00:02:47,184
and then it gets load
balance to the servers.

34
00:02:47,184 --> 00:02:50,559
So the service, we gotta add first.

35
00:02:50,559 --> 00:02:58,107
All right, so, add, edit,
delete, so ipvsadm- A.

36
00:02:58,107 --> 00:03:01,319
So next up is the protocol.

37
00:03:01,319 --> 00:03:05,662
So for the protocol,
it'll be either TCP or UDP.

38
00:03:05,662 --> 00:03:10,808
So,- t, -u, so chances are it's
going to be the TCP service.

39
00:03:10,808 --> 00:03:13,496
So we'll use that as our running example.

40
00:03:13,496 --> 00:03:16,489
So, -t.

41
00:03:16,489 --> 00:03:21,169
Next up is the service address, all right?

42
00:03:21,169 --> 00:03:24,695
So this is the publicly facing address.

43
00:03:24,695 --> 00:03:29,976
So it's in the form, host:port,

44
00:03:29,976 --> 00:03:34,033
the port part is optional.

45
00:03:34,033 --> 00:03:41,110
And a host may be one of a plain IP
address or a hostname, all right?

46
00:03:41,110 --> 00:03:47,005
And then a port may be either a plain port
number or the service name of a port.

47
00:03:47,005 --> 00:03:49,738
Here we'll just assume we're
just going to use numbers.

48
00:03:49,738 --> 00:03:55,492
So we'll say, -t for
the TCP and then the service

49
00:03:55,492 --> 00:04:00,562
address is 207.175.44.110

50
00:04:00,562 --> 00:04:06,320
as the IP address, and
then port 80 as the port.

51
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:13,140
And then the last bit is
the scheduling method.

52
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:16,508
So, the scheduling method here is,

53
00:04:16,508 --> 00:04:21,841
we would specify with -s and
then whatever the method is.

54
00:04:21,841 --> 00:04:25,618
So the method could be rr for
round robin, lc for

55
00:04:25,618 --> 00:04:29,120
lease connection, sh for source hashing.

56
00:04:29,120 --> 00:04:33,983
They have some others, but these are the
ones we covered in the last lesson, and so

57
00:04:33,983 --> 00:04:35,760
showing those as an example.

58
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:41,771
All right, so we would have -s rr.

59
00:04:41,771 --> 00:04:46,968
All right, so then the next piece
is now we have the service defined,

60
00:04:46,968 --> 00:04:51,909
we now need to define the servers or
add servers to the server set.

61
00:04:51,909 --> 00:04:58,770
So here we would have a similar command,
ipvsadm command, bunch of stuff.

62
00:04:58,770 --> 00:05:03,278
All right, so the commands, again,
would be add, edit, or delete.

63
00:05:03,278 --> 00:05:07,849
And these are just lowercase
versions of the previous,

64
00:05:07,849 --> 00:05:10,386
when we were adding a service.

65
00:05:10,386 --> 00:05:15,666
So, ipvsadmin- A, to add a server.

66
00:05:15,666 --> 00:05:21,808
So next up is the protocol and
service address parts.

67
00:05:21,808 --> 00:05:27,006
So, basically, just match whatever
was specified for adding the service.

68
00:05:27,006 --> 00:05:33,208
So here we're adding the service,
we said -t, in this address.

69
00:05:33,208 --> 00:05:37,980
So just include that again
when you're adding the server.

70
00:05:37,980 --> 00:05:42,927
Then the next part we're
going to add the server address.

71
00:05:42,927 --> 00:05:46,644
And this is the address
of the backend server.

72
00:05:46,644 --> 00:05:51,798
So for this, we would say -r and
then the server address.

73
00:05:51,798 --> 00:05:53,621
But before we add that into an example,

74
00:05:53,621 --> 00:05:56,282
we need to talk about
packet-forwarding method first.

75
00:05:56,282 --> 00:06:00,873
So let me take a quick aside here.

76
00:06:00,873 --> 00:06:04,901
Actually it's going to be, well,
so it's sort of an aside, but

77
00:06:04,901 --> 00:06:09,007
it's going to talk about this
packet-forwarding method first,

78
00:06:09,007 --> 00:06:13,433
because that influences what you
have to put for the server address.

79
00:06:13,433 --> 00:06:18,807
So, the packet-forwarding
method can be one of g,

80
00:06:18,807 --> 00:06:22,226
i, or m, for gatewaying, ip and

81
00:06:22,226 --> 00:06:26,520
ip, encapsulation, or masquerading.

82
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:32,217
So, what this really means is, what is
the address that's seen right here?

83
00:06:32,217 --> 00:06:34,645
What does this kind of look like?

84
00:06:34,645 --> 00:06:38,441
So in gatewaying, there's going to
be no change in the address.

85
00:06:38,441 --> 00:06:42,965
So this is the default,
they also call it direct routing, but

86
00:06:42,965 --> 00:06:48,022
you don't have the change,
there's no address translation here.

87
00:06:48,022 --> 00:06:53,174
Challenge with that is if you're
going to have the same IP address for

88
00:06:53,174 --> 00:06:57,866
each of the servers,
well then it's going to happen at the kind

89
00:06:57,866 --> 00:07:02,200
of Mac address level,
that something needs to change.

90
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:05,781
And so
that adds a little extra complexity.

91
00:07:05,781 --> 00:07:12,999
Ip and ip encapsulation is basically
going to tunnel to the particular server.

92
00:07:12,999 --> 00:07:18,243
And then masquerading will be using
network address translation or

93
00:07:18,243 --> 00:07:20,451
NAT to direct to the server.

94
00:07:20,451 --> 00:07:26,335
So, for the most part, just assume you're
going to be using masquerading, but feel

95
00:07:26,335 --> 00:07:32,066
free to kind of play around with these
other ones and see what they're all about.

96
00:07:32,066 --> 00:07:37,986
As an aside, let me talk about
direct server return or DSR.

97
00:07:37,986 --> 00:07:43,093
So, when a server, so
client sends a request to the server,

98
00:07:43,093 --> 00:07:45,610
the server is going to respond.

99
00:07:45,610 --> 00:07:51,448
Now, when a server replies to the request,
how is the traffic forwarded?

100
00:07:51,448 --> 00:07:55,872
So the first option is to go back
through the load balancer, all right?

101
00:07:55,872 --> 00:07:57,274
Certainly seems reasonable.

102
00:07:57,274 --> 00:08:00,900
It has all the address translation and
all that kind of stuff.

103
00:08:00,900 --> 00:08:06,347
Option 2, which is widely
used in production services,

104
00:08:06,347 --> 00:08:12,808
is to directly respond to the client,
bypassing the load balancer.

105
00:08:12,808 --> 00:08:17,022
So this keeps the load
less on the load balancer.

106
00:08:17,022 --> 00:08:20,434
So it just needs to make the translations,
but

107
00:08:20,434 --> 00:08:25,869
this requires support on the server
side to be able to handle this as well.

108
00:08:25,869 --> 00:08:30,242
But this is something called DSR or
direct server return.

109
00:08:30,242 --> 00:08:35,101
And I only want to mention that just so
that if you see this,

110
00:08:35,101 --> 00:08:37,842
you're kind of aware what it is.

111
00:08:37,842 --> 00:08:42,234
All right, so now back to adding a server,

112
00:08:42,234 --> 00:08:48,578
the server address part at
the address of the backend server,

113
00:08:48,578 --> 00:08:52,497
we would say -r real server address.

114
00:08:52,497 --> 00:08:55,284
And so with masquerading,

115
00:08:55,284 --> 00:09:00,129
the port can be different
from the service, and

116
00:09:00,129 --> 00:09:06,202
with tunneling or direct routing,
the port must be equal.

117
00:09:06,202 --> 00:09:08,302
In this example,
we're just going to keep it equal.

118
00:09:08,302 --> 00:09:12,443
We're going to use masquerading -m, and

119
00:09:12,443 --> 00:09:20,618
the real server that we're
adding is 192.168.10.1, port 80.

120
00:09:20,618 --> 00:09:24,081
All right, and then,
a final extra optional note is,

121
00:09:24,081 --> 00:09:28,072
if you have the different servers
have different capacities,

122
00:09:28,072 --> 00:09:30,119
you can add a weighting to this.

123
00:09:30,119 --> 00:09:34,142
And so you can specify -w
followed by some weight, and

124
00:09:34,142 --> 00:09:38,966
it's just an integer that specifies
the capacity of the server.

125
00:09:38,966 --> 00:09:43,292
And it'll be used in
algorithms with a weight.

126
00:09:43,292 --> 00:09:51,513
So for example, weighted round robin
would use that, round robin would not.

127
00:09:51,513 --> 00:09:56,103
All right, so, with all that as the base,
now it's time for

128
00:09:56,103 --> 00:09:58,445
you to go practice on your own.

129
00:09:58,445 --> 00:10:03,030
This is all in the GitHub repo,
provided as a vagrant file.

130
00:10:03,030 --> 00:10:05,676
It's the same vagrant file and

131
00:10:05,676 --> 00:10:10,364
the same container lab setup
that we use for iptables.

132
00:10:10,364 --> 00:10:15,173
And we're going to set up this
topology where two external nodes,

133
00:10:15,173 --> 00:10:18,123
a gateway node and two internal nodes.

134
00:10:18,123 --> 00:10:21,742
And so what I would suggest and
what we have a readme for

135
00:10:21,742 --> 00:10:27,387
with a bunch of commands that you can run,
is run a service on these internal nodes.

136
00:10:27,387 --> 00:10:33,621
We're going to use a tool called NC to be
able to kind of just do this real simply.

137
00:10:33,621 --> 00:10:38,850
And then run ipvsadm to load
balance between those, and

138
00:10:38,850 --> 00:10:44,203
do this in various configurations and
see what happens.

139
00:10:44,203 --> 00:10:50,836
And we're going to use NC on the client
side to connect to this service.

140
00:10:50,836 --> 00:10:54,625
All right, so go practice on your own and

141
00:10:54,625 --> 00:10:59,649
see how these different
commands all work together.

142
00:10:59,649 --> 00:11:02,065
And with that, we'll end here.

143
00:11:02,065 --> 00:11:02,871
Thanks.